course: dictionary
lesson: 1458
title: Old words, new clues
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words are rare. You will mostly find them in old books, poems and sea stories. When you meet one, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «Men-at-arms in casquet and corselet of steel» stood guard at the castle. Men-at-arms are soldiers and steel is metal, so a casquet must be a piece of armour worn on the head. Try it: «men-at-arms in helmet and corselet of steel» still makes sense, so casquet means helmet.
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. A casquet can also be an old spelling of «casket», a small box for jewels. Which meaning fits depends on the story. A box for jewels fits a treasure tale, but a helmet fits a castle full of soldiers. Read the whole sentence before you choose.
explain: A capital letter is a clue too. «The Casquets» with a capital C is a name, not a thing: it means dangerous rocks in the English Channel, near Alderney, with a lighthouse on them. When you are not sure, check a dictionary.
fact: Casquet is a rare, old word, found mostly in old books, poems and sea stories.
fact: «Casquet» means a light helmet, like a «casque».
fact: An old spelling of «casket» is casquet: a small box for jewels or treasure.
fact: «The Casquets» are dangerous rocks in the English Channel, near Alderney, with a lighthouse.
fact: Today we say «casket» for the box and «helmet» for the head covering.
exercise: «Men-at-arms in casquet and corselet of steel» guarded the castle. What is a casquet here? | A big guard-dog | A small box for jewels | A heavy gate that drops down | *A helmet
exercise: Sailors feared «the Casquets» in their stories. What are the Casquets? | A light helmet worn by soldiers | *Dangerous rocks with a lighthouse | A tall castle on a hill | A small box for treasure
exercise: Which word in «the hollow-sounding drawbridge, the spiky portcullis, mastiffs» means big guard-dogs? | hollow | *mastiffs | drawbridge | portcullis
test: «She opened the casquet and the jewels sparkled.» What is a casquet here? | A lighthouse | A light helmet | A stone wall | *A small box for jewels
test: Why might a sailor fear the Casquets? | A crew of pirates in old books | *Dangerous rocks in the English Channel | A box that opens by itself | A castle in the sky
exam: Jayden finds «casquet» in an old poem next to «corselet of steel» and soldiers. What does he work out it means? | A lighthouse | A small box | *A helmet | A guard-dog
